WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.533 --> 00:00:02.669
Recently, I watched this GQ video

00:00:02.669 --> 00:00:05.505
where Justin Bieber gave an inside look 
at the motorhome

00:00:05.505 --> 00:00:07.474
he spent the height of the pandemic in.

00:00:07.474 --> 00:00:10.210
“GQ, thank you so much for coming 
to check out my tour bus.”

00:00:10.443 --> 00:00:12.846
It's got a sauna, some 
questionable art

00:00:12.846 --> 00:00:15.982
and more closet space than 
a New York City apartment.

00:00:17.050 --> 00:00:19.986
It turns out this mammoth RV 
is custom-made

00:00:19.986 --> 00:00:23.156
and is estimated to cost 
close to $2 million.

00:00:23.857 --> 00:00:26.626
It's hard to imagine that this 
penthouse on wheels

00:00:26.626 --> 00:00:28.895
shares anything in common with this...

00:00:29.662 --> 00:00:32.966
an entry level RV that costs $30,000...

00:00:33.099 --> 00:00:35.502
roughly the same as a Honda Accord.

00:00:35.502 --> 00:00:39.205
Or even this, a vintage Fleetwood Bounder 
from 2001.

00:00:39.839 --> 00:00:41.574
But look at these RVs together

00:00:41.574 --> 00:00:44.477
and you might notice something 
they do share.

00:00:44.477 --> 00:00:48.348
It's the swooshes and swoops 
and they are everywhere.

00:00:49.849 --> 00:00:52.552
A few years ago, I moved from
New York City...

00:00:53.253 --> 00:00:55.488
to the Berkshires.

00:00:55.488 --> 00:00:57.991
Which is right here in western Massachusetts.

00:00:58.691 --> 00:01:01.828
I went from the land of people 
and public transit...

00:01:03.229 --> 00:01:05.331
to the land of cows and campers.

00:01:06.332 --> 00:01:07.000
Because of that

00:01:07.000 --> 00:01:10.437
I've been thinking about these 
ubiquitous swoops a lot...

00:01:10.804 --> 00:01:12.872
wondering why they've lasted so long

00:01:12.872 --> 00:01:15.141
and if they are here to stay.

00:01:15.608 --> 00:01:18.244
To try and finally get answers 
to this question...

00:01:18.611 --> 00:01:20.880
I'm going to Elkhart, Indiana:

00:01:20.880 --> 00:01:23.383
The RV capital of the world.

00:01:31.424 --> 00:01:33.393
The amount of RVs produced here 

00:01:33.393 --> 00:01:37.530
amounts to about 80% 
of global RV production.

00:01:37.864 --> 00:01:40.100
Oh, there's an RV.

00:01:40.100 --> 00:01:41.501
There's manufacturers.

00:01:41.501 --> 00:01:43.369
There's dealers. There's campgrounds.

00:01:43.369 --> 00:01:46.473
There's the RV History Museum, 
which is where I'm going.

00:01:46.806 --> 00:01:48.308
You heard that right.

00:01:48.308 --> 00:01:50.276
RVs are such a part of Elkhart’s DNA

00:01:50.276 --> 00:01:52.846
that they have a museum and Hall of Fame 
dedicated to them.

00:01:53.546 --> 00:01:56.349
[laughing] I'm so excited.

00:02:01.121 --> 00:02:02.021
Yeah.

00:02:02.856 --> 00:02:05.225
I am at the RV Museum Library.

00:02:05.225 --> 00:02:09.662
Behind me are hundreds, 
if not thousands of catalogs

00:02:09.662 --> 00:02:12.966
going back to like the 1950s and beyond.

00:02:13.199 --> 00:02:14.901
That's so cool.

00:02:14.901 --> 00:02:18.338
It seems no matter the decade, 
RVs had multicolor paid jobs

00:02:18.371 --> 00:02:21.007
but for a long time they were 
straight lines.

00:02:21.274 --> 00:02:23.376
That is just so many straight lines.

00:02:23.676 --> 00:02:26.312
I think a big driving factor 
in a lot of that...

00:02:26.713 --> 00:02:28.014
is technology.

00:02:29.482 --> 00:02:31.951
Back in the 60s and 70s, 
everything was straight.

00:02:31.951 --> 00:02:33.186
Well, everything was straight

00:02:33.186 --> 00:02:35.588
because they had tape and 
they had their hands.

00:02:35.822 --> 00:02:37.090
That's Ryan Loucks.

00:02:37.090 --> 00:02:39.726
He designs custom exterior graphics 
for Newell

00:02:39.726 --> 00:02:41.661
a high end motorhome maker.

00:02:41.661 --> 00:02:43.897
They’re in the same luxury category 
of the company

00:02:43.897 --> 00:02:45.798
that made Justin Bieber's bus.

00:02:45.798 --> 00:02:47.967
We'll get to how Ryan designs these 
in a second.

00:02:47.967 --> 00:02:50.670
But first, you have the computer 
come on the scene...

00:02:50.837 --> 00:02:53.306
and the computer can cut these 
perfect stripes

00:02:53.306 --> 00:02:54.674
and curves for you.

00:02:54.674 --> 00:02:55.875
And so, you know, you kind of

00:02:55.875 --> 00:02:57.510
have the first layer of 
printed graphics

00:02:57.510 --> 00:02:58.912
that were cut and applied.

00:02:58.912 --> 00:03:01.948
Next, evolution is a layer 
of depth and detail

00:03:01.948 --> 00:03:03.850
that just wasn't possible before.

00:03:03.850 --> 00:03:06.085
This is a 1986 Fleetwood Bounder.

00:03:06.219 --> 00:03:08.621
You might recognize it as 
the RV from Breaking Bad.

00:03:09.389 --> 00:03:11.791
Unfortunately, these stripes 
didn't last long.

00:03:12.458 --> 00:03:13.459
By the new millennium

00:03:13.459 --> 00:03:16.663
as graphic designers got more and more 
adventurous with digital tools...

00:03:16.863 --> 00:03:20.166
straight lines were out and the swooshes 
and swoops were in.

00:03:20.700 --> 00:03:22.769
This is what the Fleetwood Bounder 
looks like today.

00:03:24.037 --> 00:03:26.639
Okay, so what exactly...

00:03:27.140 --> 00:03:31.110
am I defining as swoosh and swoop?

00:03:31.211 --> 00:03:32.478
Look at this. Okay.

00:03:33.513 --> 00:03:35.248
This is a perfect example.

00:03:35.248 --> 00:03:37.083
Okay, let me get closer.

00:03:38.851 --> 00:03:39.919
This.

00:03:39.919 --> 00:03:40.987
Let's see.

00:03:41.254 --> 00:03:43.523
That is a swoosh.

00:03:44.357 --> 00:03:46.793
That is a swoop.

00:03:47.994 --> 00:03:49.128
Swoosh.

00:03:49.929 --> 00:03:51.064
Swoop.

00:03:51.164 --> 00:03:52.832
Don't fight me on that, okay?

00:03:53.733 --> 00:03:55.168
Before I got to Elkhart

00:03:55.168 --> 00:03:58.805
I chatted with Ryan about 
his custom design process.

00:03:59.505 --> 00:04:02.375
So while I clean up these books 
and put them away...

00:04:03.443 --> 00:04:05.078
you get to watch that.

00:04:05.078 --> 00:04:06.379
Cut to the interview.

00:04:07.113 --> 00:04:10.149
Generally speaking, when you're doing
paint design

00:04:10.149 --> 00:04:12.919
you want your pin color to go first.

00:04:12.952 --> 00:04:14.454
On a coach like this,

00:04:14.454 --> 00:04:16.489
the whole thing would be sprayed white...

00:04:16.623 --> 00:04:19.492
and then they'd go through 
and lay their pinstripe

00:04:19.492 --> 00:04:21.828
this kind of silvery or white edges 
to the shapes.

00:04:22.428 --> 00:04:24.264
They pull those all by hand.

00:04:24.264 --> 00:04:27.000
And that gives you kind of 
the roadmap

00:04:27.000 --> 00:04:28.735
for everything else that happens.

00:04:28.735 --> 00:04:30.103
In Newell's process...

00:04:31.004 --> 00:04:33.206
they're going to go to 
the next darkest color

00:04:33.206 --> 00:04:35.241
starting with the lightest and 
working towards dark

00:04:35.241 --> 00:04:36.276
till they get to the end

00:04:36.276 --> 00:04:38.978
which is usually the posting 
or background pattern.

00:04:39.312 --> 00:04:42.348
And ghosting is—there's kind of a color 
in the background.

00:04:42.348 --> 00:04:45.918
And then we add a layer of 
clear, translucent pearl

00:04:45.918 --> 00:04:49.155
similar to what's used in like women's 
nail polish as a topcoat.

00:04:49.355 --> 00:04:51.157
So at see through, you can see 
the color behind it

00:04:51.157 --> 00:04:52.892
but adds some sparkle

00:04:52.892 --> 00:04:54.894
and some, you know, some interest there.

00:04:55.194 --> 00:04:57.630
During this time they might also 
add drop shadows

00:04:57.630 --> 00:05:00.767
which makes the swoops and swirls
look almost three-dimensional.

00:05:00.767 --> 00:05:02.735
Which is a whole art form 
in and of itself.

00:05:02.735 --> 00:05:04.871
There's no mechanical way 
to add drop shadows.

00:05:04.871 --> 00:05:06.639
That is somebody with a paint gun

00:05:06.639 --> 00:05:08.708
almost like that guy with the pinstripe going

00:05:08.708 --> 00:05:10.710
“I have to follow this curve perfectly...

00:05:10.943 --> 00:05:13.279
with this black paint and not mess it up.”

00:05:13.279 --> 00:05:15.315
These guys are basically artists...

00:05:15.581 --> 00:05:17.550
whether they realize it about themselves or not.

00:05:18.618 --> 00:05:22.121
I just did a crazy turnaround

00:05:22.121 --> 00:05:24.157
because I just saw 
the coolest looking RV.

00:05:25.558 --> 00:05:26.893
Let me show you.

00:05:31.130 --> 00:05:33.132
Isn't that so cool?

00:05:33.666 --> 00:05:34.901
That's awesome.

00:05:34.901 --> 00:05:36.602
When I first wrote up this pitch

00:05:36.602 --> 00:05:39.372
I described the swooshes 
as mind boggling...

00:05:39.605 --> 00:05:41.641
atrocious and dated.

00:05:42.208 --> 00:05:45.445
And I found a like-minded band 
of anti-swooshers online

00:05:45.445 --> 00:05:47.747
that made me feel like I was on 
the right side of this debate.

00:05:48.348 --> 00:05:50.850
But when I came across Ryan's work, 
it was the first time

00:05:50.850 --> 00:05:54.320
where I genuinely appreciated 
the complexity and the detail.

00:05:54.320 --> 00:05:57.657
And let's be honest, the fully 
maximalist approach

00:05:57.657 --> 00:05:59.959
to graphic design that these swooshes provide.

00:06:00.660 --> 00:06:02.995
But of course, Newell’s cost $2 million

00:06:02.995 --> 00:06:05.064
and RVs like these with fully custom

00:06:05.064 --> 00:06:06.933
and very personal paint jobs

00:06:06.933 --> 00:06:10.136
make up a tiny fraction of the 11.2 million

00:06:10.136 --> 00:06:11.537
that Americans own today.

00:06:12.205 --> 00:06:13.473
Instead of multiple painters

00:06:13.473 --> 00:06:17.377
spending 195 hours on one coach 
like Ryan's team does...

00:06:17.510 --> 00:06:21.047
most new owners will get an RV with 
an automotive-grade vinyl

00:06:21.047 --> 00:06:23.316
that looks much more two-dimensional.

00:06:23.316 --> 00:06:25.985
And today, there's a good chance 
that that new RV owner

00:06:25.985 --> 00:06:27.487
is going to be a lot younger

00:06:27.487 --> 00:06:29.522
than when the swoops 
first took over.

00:06:29.522 --> 00:06:32.558
In the last 3 years, the average age 
of a first time buyer

00:06:32.558 --> 00:06:34.861
went from 41 to 32.

00:06:35.294 --> 00:06:37.296
In large part because of the pandemic

00:06:37.296 --> 00:06:40.533
when RV production and sales 
hit record highs.

00:06:40.533 --> 00:06:42.568
Just as fashion comes around

00:06:42.568 --> 00:06:44.670
just as home design comes around

00:06:44.670 --> 00:06:45.938
so does the RV industry.

00:06:45.938 --> 00:06:49.842
And we are always looking 
to stay on trend...

00:06:50.410 --> 00:06:52.278
but also design for the masses.

00:06:52.278 --> 00:06:53.946
That's Ashley Bontrager.

00:06:53.946 --> 00:06:56.916
She co-founded Ember RVs in 2021.

00:06:57.450 --> 00:07:01.587
I personally like a minimalistic approach
to design.

00:07:01.587 --> 00:07:03.022
I want it to be linear.

00:07:03.022 --> 00:07:05.291
We wanted to enhance the design

00:07:05.291 --> 00:07:08.161
of our units, like the shapes 
and the windows.

00:07:08.294 --> 00:07:11.497
You know, one of the things we said is
we want to make them appear larger.

00:07:11.497 --> 00:07:14.500
So we worked—on our designer worked on 
black graphics

00:07:14.500 --> 00:07:16.436
that just extend the length of the window

00:07:16.436 --> 00:07:18.805
to make them appear larger from afar.

00:07:19.172 --> 00:07:22.008
Ember makes RVs that are part of 
a growing category

00:07:22.008 --> 00:07:23.609
called overlanders.

00:07:23.609 --> 00:07:26.479
They're designed to go 
off-road and off-grid.

00:07:26.746 --> 00:07:28.681
Looking at our RVs across this category

00:07:28.681 --> 00:07:31.417
you start to notice that
there's no swoops.

00:07:31.951 --> 00:07:34.187
Though some do look like 
straight up tanks.

00:07:34.687 --> 00:07:36.722
And it turns out a less swoopy look

00:07:36.722 --> 00:07:40.860
has been percolating amongst other RV designs
the last few years.

00:07:40.860 --> 00:07:44.464
Ultimately, I think you will see a design trend...

00:07:44.697 --> 00:07:46.265
going more toward this

00:07:46.265 --> 00:07:49.235
more minimal, linear graphics 
in the RV space.

00:07:49.802 --> 00:07:53.072
For every swoopy RV I saw 
while driving around Elkhart

00:07:53.072 --> 00:07:57.310
I also saw edgier designs with names like 
Raptor and Avenger.

00:07:57.743 --> 00:07:59.145
Even the iconic Bounder

00:07:59.145 --> 00:08:01.614
has straightened up their lines for 2024.

00:08:01.981 --> 00:08:04.851
Perhaps we're moving from 
the era of swoops to daggers.

00:08:05.117 --> 00:08:07.019
I'll let you decide if that's a good thing.

00:08:07.019 --> 00:08:09.922
So I have some people that say 
“I love the swoops and swirls.”

00:08:09.922 --> 00:08:10.990
“They've always been there.”

00:08:10.990 --> 00:08:12.925
“It makes me feel like home.”

00:08:12.925 --> 00:08:14.093
“Give me tradition.”

00:08:14.093 --> 00:08:16.262
But for every person that says that

00:08:16.262 --> 00:08:17.630
you've got somebody that says

00:08:17.630 --> 00:08:20.166
“I am so sick of the swoops and swirls.”

00:08:20.433 --> 00:08:22.101
“Let's do something different.”

